window.PLAN={"months":[{"n":1,"title":"Open the door","from":1,"to":30,"intro":"The goal this month is a routine, not a hit: 30 posts out and a session that takes 20 minutes. Practice focus is shell voicings and your first hours on Push 2. Everything is short and forgiving on purpose.","howto":"How-to of the month: finding the pitch of an object. Record the sound on your phone, loop it, and hum along until your hum sits inside it. Find that hum on the keys. If it falls between two keys, pick the nearer one and say so on camera; honesty about it is part of the charm."},{"n":2,"title":"Find the voice","from":31,"to":61,"intro":"Your voice is the instrument you already play best, so this month every series leans on it. Practice focus is three-part harmony and clean takes. By Day 61 you should be able to stack a small choir in ten minutes.","howto":"How-to of the month: stacking harmonies fast. Record the melody. Duplicate the track twice. On the first copy sing a third above; on the second, a fifth above or a third below. Pan them left and right, lower each by 4 dB under the lead, and send all three to one reverb."},{"n":3,"title":"Push 2 from zero","from":62,"to":91,"intro":"This month the glowing grid is in every frame, because a beginner visibly getting better at an unusual instrument is a story people follow. Practice focus is Drum Rack, clips, scenes and finger drumming. Day 91 is your first full performance with Push, keys and voice together.","howto":"How-to of the month: any sound into a drum. Record the sound into an audio track. Trim it to the hit. Drag it onto an empty Drum Rack pad. Shorten the decay in Simpler until it is tight. Three everyday sounds plus one kick make a usable kit."},{"n":4,"title":"Gospel chords school","from":92,"to":122,"intro":"Gospel harmony is where your theory knowledge pays off on camera, and chord lessons collect saves for months. Practice focus is extended voicings and passing chords, and you open Logic for the first time. The Friday Finish grows to 60 seconds or more from here.","howto":"How-to of the month: reharmonising any simple tune. Keep the melody on top. Under each long melody note, try three chords that contain it. Put a passing diminished chord a semitone below any chord you want to arrive at. Add a 9th to every major and minor chord."},{"n":5,"title":"Blues month","from":123,"to":152,"intro":"The blues is the most forgiving form for a developing player and the funniest for everyday complaints, which makes it your most sendable month. Practice focus is the twelve-bar form in four keys, the shuffle feel and three licks you can play without thinking.","howto":"How-to of the month: writing a blues verse in two minutes. Line one states the problem. Line two repeats it with a small change. Line three answers or twists it and carries the rhyme. Sing it over twelve bars: four bars per line."},{"n":6,"title":"First EP in public","from":153,"to":183,"intro":"Four of your best fragments become finished songs this month, and followers watch every decision. Practice focus is writing complete songs and finishing them in Logic. The EP goes out on Day 180 as free streaming; a distributor such as DistroKid or TuneCore charges a small fee, and posting the full tracks on Instagram and YouTube costs nothing.","howto":"How-to of the month: fragment to song. Your 30-second piece is the chorus. Write a verse that explains how you got there, in a lower register. Write a bridge that says the opposite or asks a question. Order: verse, chorus, verse, chorus, bridge, chorus. Aim for two and a half minutes."},{"n":7,"title":"Three languages","from":184,"to":213,"intro":"Singing soul and gospel in Hindi, Telugu and English is the thing very few accounts can copy, so this month puts it at the centre. Practice focus is lyric writing and diction in all three, plus Indian rhythmic and melodic ideas set against Western harmony.","howto":"How-to of the month: one chorus, three languages. Write the idea in the language it came to you in. Translate the meaning, not the words, into the other two. Count syllables per line and adjust the melody's rhythm for each language instead of forcing the words to fit."},{"n":8,"title":"Collaboration season","from":214,"to":244,"intro":"Collab posts put your Reel in front of another creator's audience, which is the fastest legitimate growth lever on the platform. Practice focus is accompanying others and the file discipline that makes remote work painless. Line up four or five partners in the last week of Month 7, starting with accounts near your own size.","howto":"How-to of the month: a clean remote collab. Send a WAV of the instrumental at a stated tempo and key, with two bars of click at the front. Ask for a dry WAV back, recorded to that file. Agree in writing who posts, that it is a collab post with both names, and that either of you may use the audio. Always get permission before featuring anyone's voice or video."},{"n":9,"title":"Easy jazz","from":245,"to":274,"intro":"Easy jazz gives the account its late-evening mood and gives you the voicings that make every other style sound richer. Practice focus is two-five-ones in all twelve keys, rootless left-hand voicings and a relaxed swing feel. Keep everything slow; slow jazz hides a developing technique and suits the genre.","howto":"How-to of the month: a rootless voicing. For a major 7th chord, the left hand plays 3, 5, 7 and 9 and leaves the root out. For a dominant chord, play 7, 9, 3 and 13. The bass, or your imagination, supplies the root. Practise them through a two-five-one in one new key each day."},{"n":10,"title":"The live set","from":275,"to":305,"intro":"By now you have the pieces; this month you learn to perform them in one take and take them to Instagram Live. Practice focus is live looping on Push and singing while playing without looking down. Go live every Sunday; the Sunday row is the best minute cut from that session.","howto":"How-to of the month: a live-loop song. In Ableton, set four tracks with fixed-length loops of four or eight bars: beat, bass, chords, vocals. Record each in that order from Push, one pass each. Then sing the lead over the top. Rehearse the order until you can do it without thinking about buttons."},{"n":11,"title":"Ballad season","from":306,"to":335,"intro":"Ballads are where people attach a song to someone they love, which is why they get sent and saved. Practice focus is arrangement, dynamics and mixing a vocal so it sounds close. Anything drawn from a follower's personal story is used only with their permission and without identifying details.","howto":"How-to of the month: arranging a ballad. Verse one is piano and voice only. Add a pad or oohs in the first chorus. Add bass in verse two. Bring strings and harmonies for the second chorus. Strip back to piano and voice for the final line. The arrangement is a single slow rise and one drop."},{"n":12,"title":"The year-one record","from":336,"to":365,"intro":"The last month turns the year into a finished record and a finale people have been counting down to. Practice focus is polishing: re-recording early pieces and finishing mixes. Before-and-after comparisons with your own early days are the strongest content you will make all year.","howto":"How-to of the month: a side-by-side. Put the old clip on the left and film the new one in the same frame and key on the right. Play the old audio for four seconds, then the new. Do not comment on the old one; the difference speaks."}],"rows":[{"d":1,"s":"Moment","make":"Record your pressure cooker whistle, find its pitch on the keys, and play four gospel chords underneath that make the whistle the melody note.","hook":"My pressure cooker whistles in B flat.","m":1},{"d":2,"s":"Theory","make":"Play C major, then Cmaj7, then Cmaj9, holding each for two bars. Label each on screen. Ask which one viewers feel most.","hook":"Same chord. Three levels of feeling.","m":1},{"d":3,"s":"Push","make":"Switch on Push 2, choose Note mode, In Key, C minor. Play one-finger melodies on the pads. Show your uncertain face; that is the story.","hook":"Day 3 with a machine I can't play.","m":1},{"d":4,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing one short line. Record it again as a double. Add a third above. Reveal each layer with a label.","hook":"One voice in. Three voices out.","m":1},{"d":5,"s":"Finish","make":"Combine Day 1's chords and Day 4's stack into a 30-second piece called Whistle. Start the Reel with the raw cooker clip.","hook":"A week ago this was kitchen noise.","m":1},{"d":6,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play three sounds from your home for three seconds each: fan, tap, gate. Ask viewers to vote in comments for the one you turn into music.","hook":"Pick one. I'll make it a song.","m":1},{"d":7,"s":"Still","make":"One take of Amazing Grace, slow, with major 7th chords in the left hand. No cuts, no text after the first frame.","hook":"No edits. Just Sunday.","m":1},{"d":8,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a railway station announcement chime. Play it back, then answer each note with a chord until it becomes a four-bar tune.","hook":"The station chime was always a song.","m":1},{"d":9,"s":"Theory","make":"Show the Amen cadence: the IV chord falling to I. Play it plain, then with a 6th added. Explain why hymns end this way.","hook":"Why every hymn ends like this.","m":1},{"d":10,"s":"Push","make":"Load a Drum Rack. Use the step sequencer to place kick, clap and hat. Film the pads lighting up from above.","hook":"Day 10. I made a beat by pressing squares.","m":1},{"d":11,"s":"Stack","make":"Hum a bass line into the NT1, then pitch it down an octave in Ableton. Sing a melody over your own bass.","hook":"My bass player is also me.","m":1},{"d":12,"s":"Finish","make":"Build the station chime tune into 40 seconds with the Day 10 beat and Day 11 bass. Open on the real chime.","hook":"Platform 2 co-wrote this one.","m":1},{"d":13,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Deliver the sound voters chose on Day 6. Show the winning comment on screen, then the object, then the music it became.","hook":"You picked it. Here is your song.","m":1},{"d":14,"s":"Still","make":"Write a four-line chorus of gratitude just before filming. Sing it once at the piano. Keep the first take.","hook":"I wrote this ten minutes ago.","m":1},{"d":15,"s":"Moment","make":"Play your phone's alarm tone, then reharmonise the same notes as a lullaby: slow tempo, soft Rhodes, major 7ths.","hook":"Making my alarm less evil.","m":1},{"d":16,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a sus4 chord and hold it. Wait. Resolve to the major chord. Repeat with sus2. Let the silence do the work.","hook":"This chord is holding its breath.","m":1},{"d":17,"s":"Push","make":"Record your first looping clip on Push, then overdub a second layer while it plays. Show the moment the loop comes round.","hook":"I pressed record and it kept going.","m":1},{"d":18,"s":"Stack","make":"Layer a low hum, a mid ooh and a whispered octave above to make a pad. Play it back under a piano note.","hook":"This synth pad is 100 percent throat.","m":1},{"d":19,"s":"Finish","make":"Finish the alarm lullaby: 35 seconds, vocal pad from Day 18, no drums. End on the original alarm, now gentle.","hook":"The lullaby version of 6 a.m.","m":1},{"d":20,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask viewers to comment any two notes. Demonstrate with your own pair: play both, then a melody that travels between them.","hook":"Comment two notes. I'll connect them.","m":1},{"d":21,"s":"Still","make":"A slow 6/8 gospel instrumental, one take. Count the six beats aloud for the first bar, then just play.","hook":"Six beats, no hurry.","m":1},{"d":22,"s":"Moment","make":"Film chai being poured and stirred. Tap the tempo of the spoon, set the metronome to it, and play a groove at that speed.","hook":"Chai, at 92 beats per minute.","m":1},{"d":23,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a chord with a close left hand, then spread it: root, fifth and tenth. Alternate the two so the difference is obvious.","hook":"Your left hand is why it sounds thin.","m":1},{"d":24,"s":"Push","make":"Keep one finger pattern on the pads and change only the scale setting: Dorian, Phrygian, major pentatonic. Label each.","hook":"Same fingers. I changed one setting.","m":1},{"d":25,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a question on the left channel and answer it on the right. Three exchanges, harmonised on the last.","hook":"Arguing with myself, in harmony.","m":1},{"d":26,"s":"Finish","make":"Turn the chai groove into 40 seconds with Push drums and the call-and-response from Day 25.","hook":"Four ingredients. One of them is tea.","m":1},{"d":27,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play an untitled 20-second instrumental. Ask for a name in the comments and promise the winner's title goes on the file.","hook":"This piece needs a name. Yours.","m":1},{"d":28,"s":"Still","make":"An original Hindi worship chorus, one take, eyes closed if that is natural. English translation as small subtitles.","hook":"One take. One prayer.","m":1},{"d":29,"s":"Moment","make":"Record your ceiling fan's hum, find its note, and play a slow blues lick over the hum as a drone.","hook":"My fan hums an A. So I joined in.","m":1},{"d":30,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the four chord types you learned this month in order from plain to rich. Put a ranking number on each.","hook":"Ranking this month's chords by goosebumps.","m":1},{"d":31,"s":"Push","make":"Record yourself singing one long aah. Drop it into Simpler on Push and play your own voice as chords on the pads.","hook":"I turned one aah into an instrument.","m":2},{"d":32,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a single word on the root, then add the 3rd, then the 5th, with a label for each floor of the chord.","hook":"Building a choir, one floor at a time.","m":2},{"d":33,"s":"Finish","make":"A 30-second piece made only of your voice and the Day 31 voice instrument. No piano, no drums.","hook":"Nothing here but my throat.","m":2},{"d":34,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask for one word in the comments. Show the format with your own example: sing the word love as a four-part chord.","hook":"Give me a word. I'll give it harmony.","m":2},{"d":35,"s":"Still","make":"Sing a verse of It Is Well with only one repeated piano note under it. The space is the arrangement.","hook":"One note under an old hymn.","m":2},{"d":36,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a street vendor's call. Find the interval in it, name it on screen, then harmonise the call into a short refrain.","hook":"The vegetable seller sings a minor third.","m":2},{"d":37,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing one phrase in chest voice, then the same phrase in head voice. Explain in one caption line where each belongs.","hook":"Same note. Two different voices.","m":2},{"d":38,"s":"Push","make":"Slice a sung phrase to a Drum Rack so each pad holds one syllable. Replay the syllables as a new rhythm.","hook":"I cut my voice into 16 pieces.","m":2},{"d":39,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a melody, then add a line a sixth below all the way through. Compare it with thirds so viewers hear the difference.","hook":"The harmony nobody tries: sixths.","m":2},{"d":40,"s":"Finish","make":"Turn the vendor's call into a 40-second soul piece with the sixths harmony and the chopped-voice rhythm.","hook":"He was selling tomatoes. I heard a chorus.","m":2},{"d":41,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Take three words from Day 34's comments and sing each as a different chord quality: major, minor, dominant 7th.","hook":"You sent words. I sent chords back.","m":2},{"d":42,"s":"Still","make":"Hum a wordless lullaby over slow piano. Tell viewers in the caption it has no words yet and ask what it is about.","hook":"A lullaby with no words yet.","m":2},{"d":43,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a real yawn. Loop it, notice the falling glide, then sing that glide as a soul run and land it on a chord.","hook":"My yawn is a perfect soul run.","m":2},{"d":44,"s":"Theory","make":"Hold a straight note for four beats, then add vibrato for four. Show one phrase where straight is better.","hook":"When to shake a note, and when not.","m":2},{"d":45,"s":"Push","make":"Put Ableton's Vocoder on your vocal track with a synth as the carrier. Speak a sentence while holding chords.","hook":"My keyboard learned to talk.","m":2},{"d":46,"s":"Stack","make":"Stack a major chord with three voices. Re-record only the middle voice a semitone lower to make minor, then lower the top for diminished.","hook":"Moving one singer changes everything.","m":2},{"d":47,"s":"Finish","make":"Build the yawn run into a 35-second slow jam. Keep the real yawn as the first sound.","hook":"This started as a yawn. Honestly.","m":2},{"d":48,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Post eight bars of instrumental with an empty space where a verse should be. Invite singers to remix it with their own.","hook":"This verse is empty. It's yours.","m":2},{"d":49,"s":"Still","make":"Sing an original spiritual-style melody with only foot taps for time. Film your foot and your face in one frame.","hook":"Just a voice and a foot.","m":2},{"d":50,"s":"Moment","make":"Say an everyday family question the way it is said at home, such as khana khaya? Play its speech melody on the keys, then harmonise it.","hook":"My mother's question is a melody.","m":2},{"d":51,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing a line and run out of breath on purpose. Then mark two breath points on screen and sing it again.","hook":"You're running out of air right here.","m":2},{"d":52,"s":"Push","make":"Reverse a sung word, add reverb, freeze and reverse it back so the reverb swells in before the word.","hook":"The ghost before the word.","m":2},{"d":53,"s":"Stack","make":"Arrange a four-part ending tag: low bass, two middle voices, falsetto on top. Hold the last chord as long as you can.","hook":"Four of me end a song.","m":2},{"d":54,"s":"Finish","make":"Finish the family-question song: 45 seconds, the question as the chorus, the four-part tag as the ending.","hook":"A whole song from one question.","m":2},{"d":55,"s":"Your Turn","make":"With permission, feature two or three of the best open-verse replies from Day 48 as a collab post.","hook":"Strangers finished my song.","m":2},{"d":56,"s":"Still","make":"An original English worship chorus, sung quietly, with the lyric on screen so people can sing along.","hook":"For anyone who needs a quiet minute.","m":2},{"d":57,"s":"Moment","make":"Ring your doorbell on camera. Take its two notes and build a call-and-response between doorbell and voice.","hook":"My doorbell knows two notes. Enough.","m":2},{"d":58,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing a five-note pentatonic run on one syllable at half speed, then three-quarter speed, then full speed.","hook":"A five-note run, at learner speed.","m":2},{"d":59,"s":"Push","make":"Make a harmony by pitch-shifting copies of your lead by +3 and +7 semitones. Then sing the real parts. Play both.","hook":"Fake harmony or real? Can you hear it?","m":2},{"d":60,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a short melody, then enter again two bars later with the same melody as a round. Add a third entry.","hook":"Chasing myself through a melody.","m":2},{"d":61,"s":"Finish","make":"A 60-second choir piece built from the doorbell motif, with every stacking trick from the month. Count the voices on screen.","hook":"Day 61. The choir is still just me.","m":2},{"d":62,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask for a tempo and a feeling in the comments. Show an example: 70 bpm and homesick, built as a four-bar beat on Push.","hook":"Give me a tempo and a feeling.","m":3},{"d":63,"s":"Still","make":"Load an electric piano on Push, In Key mode, and play slow chords on the pads in one take.","hook":"Sunday, played on squares.","m":3},{"d":64,"s":"Moment","make":"Shake your house keys into the mic, drop the recording onto a Drum Rack pad, and program it where the hi-hat goes.","hook":"My house keys are now a hi-hat.","m":3},{"d":65,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the same beat fully quantised, then with 57 percent swing. Switch between them every two bars with a label.","hook":"The setting that makes beats feel human.","m":3},{"d":66,"s":"Push","make":"Make four clips in Session view: drums, bass, chords, melody. Launch scenes in order to show a verse becoming a chorus.","hook":"Four buttons. A whole arrangement.","m":3},{"d":67,"s":"Stack","make":"Record a vocal loop straight from Push without touching the laptop. Add two more layers the same way.","hook":"Looping my voice, hands off the laptop.","m":3},{"d":68,"s":"Finish","make":"A 40-second groove using the house-keys hi-hat, the Day 66 scenes and the Day 67 vocal loops.","hook":"The hi-hat in this lives on my key ring.","m":3},{"d":69,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Deliver the tempo-and-feeling request with the most likes from Day 62. Put the comment on screen first.","hook":"You ordered slow and heartbroken.","m":3},{"d":70,"s":"Still","make":"Hold one pad chord on Push and sing a short prayer over it. Nothing changes except your voice.","hook":"A prayer over one held chord.","m":3},{"d":71,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a dripping tap. Measure its tempo, lock Ableton to it, and play piano in time with the drips.","hook":"The leaking tap keeps better time than me.","m":3},{"d":72,"s":"Theory","make":"Show a triad shape on the Push grid, then slide the same shape to three other positions. Same shape, new chord each time.","hook":"On this grid every chord is one shape.","m":3},{"d":73,"s":"Push","make":"Finger-drum a kick and snare pattern for eight bars. Show the first failed take for two seconds, then the best one.","hook":"Take 1 versus take 19.","m":3},{"d":74,"s":"Stack","make":"Record boots, cats and tss sounds with your mouth. Load them into a Drum Rack and play a beat on your own mouth kit.","hook":"Every drum here came out of my mouth.","m":3},{"d":75,"s":"Finish","make":"Build the dripping-tap idea into 40 seconds with the mouth kit underneath. Keep the tap audible throughout.","hook":"Plumber booked. Song finished first.","m":3},{"d":76,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask followers to DM a three-second voice note of any sound. Show the format with one a friend sent you.","hook":"Your voice notes are about to be drums.","m":3},{"d":77,"s":"Still","make":"Play Be Thou My Vision on a soft Rhodes sound using the pads. One take, slow, no singing.","hook":"An old Irish tune on new pads.","m":3},{"d":78,"s":"Moment","make":"Record traffic horns from your window. Identify the interval between two horns on the keys and jam around it.","hook":"The traffic outside is playing a tritone.","m":3},{"d":79,"s":"Theory","make":"Put a compressor on the chords, sidechained to the kick. Turn it on and off every two bars.","hook":"Why the chords duck when the kick hits.","m":3},{"d":80,"s":"Push","make":"Record a filter sweep with one Push encoder over eight bars. Film the hand on the knob and the screen above it.","hook":"One knob. Instant build-up.","m":3},{"d":81,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a held note through Beat Repeat and trigger the stutter live from Push.","hook":"Glitching my own voice, on purpose.","m":3},{"d":82,"s":"Finish","make":"Turn the traffic interval into a 45-second piece with the filter build and vocal stutter. Start with the real horns.","hook":"Rush hour, rearranged.","m":3},{"d":83,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play two ten-second beats labelled A and B. Viewers vote in comments; the loser is deleted on camera next week.","hook":"Two beats. You choose which one lives.","m":3},{"d":84,"s":"Still","make":"Switch Push off on camera. Play piano alone for a minute.","hook":"No machines today.","m":3},{"d":85,"s":"Moment","make":"Record yourself typing an email. Use the typing as the hi-hat pattern and build a groove around your real typing rhythm.","hook":"Answering emails in 4/4.","m":3},{"d":86,"s":"Theory","make":"Tap three against two on two pads, slowly, counting aloud. Then speed it up until it grooves.","hook":"Three against two, finally explained.","m":3},{"d":87,"s":"Push","make":"Use the melodic step sequencer. Enter notes with your eyes closed, then open them and keep whatever the scale made of it.","hook":"I let the grid write the melody.","m":3},{"d":88,"s":"Stack","make":"Whisper ts, k and puh for drums and sing soft chords above them for a lo-fi soul sketch.","hook":"Whispering the drums.","m":3},{"d":89,"s":"Finish","make":"Finish the winning beat from Day 83 as a full 45-second piece. Delete the losing project file at the end.","hook":"You voted. I finished it.","m":3},{"d":90,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Answer the three most common questions about learning Push while playing a loop underneath.","hook":"Ninety days in. Ask me anything.","m":3},{"d":91,"s":"Still","make":"A one-take performance using Push drums, keys and voice together for the first time. Leave small mistakes in.","hook":"Three months ago I couldn't do this.","m":3},{"d":92,"s":"Moment","make":"Play Happy Birthday plain, then with gospel passing chords and a big ending. Tell viewers to send it to whoever plays at family parties.","hook":"Never play Happy Birthday plain again.","m":4},{"d":93,"s":"Theory","make":"Play chord I to chord ii, then insert a diminished chord between them. Show the three chords on screen.","hook":"The chord between the chords.","m":4},{"d":94,"s":"Push","make":"Open Logic for the first time. Add a Drummer track in 6/8 under gospel chords and react honestly to how fast it works.","hook":"Logic gave me a drummer who never tires.","m":4},{"d":95,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing Amen seven different ways: plain, with a 6th, minor, with a run, stacked, and two of your own. Number them.","hook":"Seven ways to sing Amen.","m":4},{"d":96,"s":"Finish","make":"The full gospel birthday arrangement, 60 seconds, with a stacked choir and the Logic drummer.","hook":"The birthday song, church edition.","m":4},{"d":97,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask for a nursery rhyme in the comments. Show eight bars of your own example to set the standard.","hook":"Give me a nursery rhyme. Watch this.","m":4},{"d":98,"s":"Still","make":"Blessed Assurance in one take, with 9ths and a walk-up into the chorus.","hook":"A hymn from 1873, with new chords.","m":4},{"d":99,"s":"Moment","make":"Film the microwave counting down. When it beeps, match the pitch and launch into fast gospel shout chords.","hook":"Microwave's done. Church starts.","m":4},{"d":100,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a two-five-one in C with 9ths and 13ths, slowly, naming each chord. Mark the milestone in the caption.","hook":"Day 100. Three chords behind everything.","m":4},{"d":101,"s":"Push","make":"Load Logic's tonewheel organ. Pull drawbars one at a time on screen until it sounds like a church.","hook":"Finding the church organ in my laptop.","m":4},{"d":102,"s":"Stack","make":"Record soprano, alto and tenor lines on one hallelujah. Solo each part in turn with its name on screen.","hook":"Soprano, alto, tenor. All me.","m":4},{"d":103,"s":"Finish","make":"A 45-second praise break built on the microwave beep, with organ and the three-part choir.","hook":"Dinner was ready. I wasn't.","m":4},{"d":104,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Deliver the nursery rhyme chosen on Day 97, fully reharmonised. Show the comment, then play.","hook":"Your nursery rhyme went to church.","m":4},{"d":105,"s":"Still","make":"Sing verse one of a new original worship song. Say it is unfinished and that verse two comes next Sunday.","hook":"Verse one of something new.","m":4},{"d":106,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a lift's arrival ding. Use the ding as the first note of a phrase and answer it with chords.","hook":"The lift goes ding in E. I answered.","m":4},{"d":107,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a two-five-one, then replace the five chord with the chord a tritone away. Alternate the two versions.","hook":"Swap one chord. Sound expensive.","m":4},{"d":108,"s":"Push","make":"Layer piano with a quiet pad and a sub bass in Logic. Mute and unmute the layers to show what each adds.","hook":"Why my piano sounds bigger than it is.","m":4},{"d":109,"s":"Stack","make":"Loop a simple choir phrase and sing three kinds of ad-lib over it: an echo, a run, a held high note.","hook":"What the lead does over the choir.","m":4},{"d":110,"s":"Finish","make":"The lift ding becomes a 60-second gospel piece with layered piano and ad-libs.","hook":"Going up.","m":4},{"d":111,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Post a 6/8 gospel instrumental with chords on screen and invite singers and preachers to remix it.","hook":"Six-eight groove. Bring your voice.","m":4},{"d":112,"s":"Still","make":"Come Thou Fount, one take, with a key change before the last verse.","hook":"Written in 1758. Played like tonight.","m":4},{"d":113,"s":"Moment","make":"Film a wall clock ticking. Confirm it is 60 bpm and play a slow ballad progression exactly in time with it.","hook":"My wall clock is a 60 bpm metronome.","m":4},{"d":114,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the bass walk-up from chord I to chord IV: 1, 2, flat 3, 3. Left hand alone, then with chords.","hook":"The bass walk every gospel pianist uses.","m":4},{"d":115,"s":"Push","make":"Show a note you sang flat. Open Flex Pitch in Logic, correct it on screen, and play before and after.","hook":"I sang this flat. Here's the fix.","m":4},{"d":116,"s":"Stack","make":"Take a stacked chorus and lift the whole thing up a semitone using a pivot chord. Show the moment of the lift.","hook":"The key change that lifts the room.","m":4},{"d":117,"s":"Finish","make":"A 60-second ballad at clock tempo, ending with the Day 116 key change. Keep the tick audible in the intro.","hook":"Sixty seconds, measured by my wall.","m":4},{"d":118,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play three chords without showing your hands. Viewers guess the quality in comments; reveal answers in a pinned comment.","hook":"Can you name this chord by ear?","m":4},{"d":119,"s":"Still","make":"An original lament in a minor key. Quiet, slow, unresolved at the end.","hook":"Not every Sunday song is happy.","m":4},{"d":120,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a kettle coming to the boil. Follow its rising pitch with a rising chord sequence and land when it clicks off.","hook":"The kettle rises. So does the chord.","m":4},{"d":121,"s":"Theory","make":"Hold one melody note in the right hand and play five different chords under it. Number them one to five.","hook":"One note. Five chords under it.","m":4},{"d":122,"s":"Push","make":"Take a vocal from this month, apply one EQ cut in the low mids, and switch it on and off.","hook":"Before and after one EQ move.","m":4},{"d":123,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a major third, then slide up into it from a semitone below. Stack two voices doing the slide together.","hook":"The note between the notes.","m":5},{"d":124,"s":"Finish","make":"A twelve-bar blues about your Wi-Fi dropping, with three verses and a piano fill after each line.","hook":"The Wi-Fi went down. I wrote a blues.","m":5},{"d":125,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask viewers for their smallest daily problem. Sing a sample verse about a missing sock.","hook":"Your tiniest problem deserves a blues.","m":5},{"d":126,"s":"Still","make":"A slow blues piano solo in one take, lamp only, no singing.","hook":"Slow blues, lights low.","m":5},{"d":127,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a squeaky door. Copy its slide on the keys with the pitch wheel so the door and the keyboard trade phrases.","hook":"My door plays slide guitar.","m":5},{"d":128,"s":"Theory","make":"Show the twelve-bar form as twelve boxes on screen that light up while you play through it once.","hook":"Twelve bars. That's the whole map.","m":5},{"d":129,"s":"Push","make":"Set the Push step sequencer to a triplet grid and program a shuffle. Play it straight first so the change is clear.","hook":"Teaching a grid to shuffle.","m":5},{"d":130,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a line, sing it again harmonised, then answer it. Label the lines A, A and B.","hook":"Say it. Say it again. Then answer.","m":5},{"d":131,"s":"Finish","make":"The squeaky door blues, 45 seconds, with the shuffle beat and an AAB verse about the door itself.","hook":"I should oil this door. I won't.","m":5},{"d":132,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Sing three of the problems from Day 125, ten seconds each, with the commenter's handle on screen.","hook":"Three of your problems, set to twelve bars.","m":5},{"d":133,"s":"Still","make":"Wade in the Water, voice and piano, one take, with a low left-hand riff.","hook":"A spiritual older than recording.","m":5},{"d":134,"s":"Moment","make":"Film yourself waiting on hold with customer care. Play along with the hold tone, then sing a verse about the wait.","hook":"Forty minutes on hold. Here's the song.","m":5},{"d":135,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the six-note blues scale, then three licks built from it, each shown slowly once and at speed once.","hook":"Six notes. Endless complaints.","m":5},{"d":136,"s":"Push","make":"Take a clean electric piano and add tremolo and an amp simulator. Toggle the effects so viewers hear the change.","hook":"Making a clean piano sound smoky.","m":5},{"d":137,"s":"Stack","make":"Hum and moan a wordless melody over a blues riff, stacked in two voices. No lyrics at all.","hook":"No lyrics. Still the blues.","m":5},{"d":138,"s":"Finish","make":"The on-hold blues, 50 seconds, with the smoky keys and a spoken line over the last bars.","hook":"Your call is important to us.","m":5},{"d":139,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play four bars, leave four bars empty with a count on screen, repeat. Invite players to fill the gaps.","hook":"Four bars for me. Four for you.","m":5},{"d":140,"s":"Still","make":"An original prayer sung over slow blues changes. Gospel words, blues harmony.","hook":"A prayer in a blues key.","m":5},{"d":141,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a pigeon cooing at the window. Notate its rhythm on screen and turn the three-note pattern into a piano riff.","hook":"The pigeon at my window has a riff.","m":5},{"d":142,"s":"Theory","make":"Play I, IV and V all as dominant 7ths and explain in one line why that breaks classical rules and works anyway.","hook":"The chord that breaks theory class.","m":5},{"d":143,"s":"Push","make":"Record a blues riff without quantising, then duplicate and quantise it. Play both and ask which feels better.","hook":"Quantised or human. Which one grooves?","m":5},{"d":144,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a clean line, then add grit with a saturation plugin instead of forcing your throat. Compare the two.","hook":"Grit without wrecking my throat.","m":5},{"d":145,"s":"Finish","make":"The pigeon riff blues, 45 seconds, unquantised, with a gritty double on the last line.","hook":"Co-written with a bird.","m":5},{"d":146,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Sing the first two lines of a blues verse and stop. Ask viewers to write the third line in the comments.","hook":"I wrote two lines. You write the third.","m":5},{"d":147,"s":"Still","make":"Your first boogie-woogie left hand, played slowly in one take, with a simple right-hand melody.","hook":"My left hand's first boogie.","m":5},{"d":148,"s":"Moment","make":"During a power cut, or with the lights off, film by candle or phone torch and play an unplugged-feeling blues.","hook":"Power cut. Candle on. Blues time.","m":5},{"d":149,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the last two bars of a blues three ways: a simple V chord, a chromatic walk-down, and a gospel turnaround.","hook":"Three ways to end a blues verse.","m":5},{"d":150,"s":"Push","make":"Program a walking bass line in Logic, then play one live with your left hand. Put them side by side.","hook":"Left hand versus laptop: who walks better?","m":5},{"d":151,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing the three best third lines from Day 146, harmonised, with each writer credited on screen.","hook":"Your lines, sung. Credit on screen.","m":5},{"d":152,"s":"Finish","make":"A 75-second medley joining the Wi-Fi, door, hold-music and pigeon riffs into one blues.","hook":"A month of small problems. One song.","m":5},{"d":153,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play five-second clips of six past fragments, numbered. Viewers vote for the four that become full songs.","hook":"Four of these become real songs. Vote.","m":6},{"d":154,"s":"Still","make":"Play the top-voted fragment as a full-length rough draft at the piano, humming where words are missing.","hook":"The long version, first draft.","m":6},{"d":155,"s":"Moment","make":"Film your pen writing a lyric. Record the scratch of the pen and use its rhythm as percussion under the line you wrote.","hook":"The sound of a lyric being written.","m":6},{"d":156,"s":"Theory","make":"Draw verse, chorus and bridge as boxes on paper. Point to each while playing a few seconds of it.","hook":"Why your idea isn't a song yet.","m":6},{"d":157,"s":"Push","make":"Record five vocal takes of one line in Logic. Show the comp tool choosing the best words from each.","hook":"Five bad takes make one good one.","m":6},{"d":158,"s":"Stack","make":"Try a third above and a fifth above on the EP chorus. Play both and tell viewers which one you kept.","hook":"Which harmony stays? Third or fifth?","m":6},{"d":159,"s":"Finish","make":"The finished chorus of song one, 45 seconds, with production. Title on screen.","hook":"Song one has a chorus.","m":6},{"d":160,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play the verse and chorus of song one, stop where the bridge should be, and ask for lyric ideas on its theme.","hook":"I'm stuck on the bridge. Help.","m":6},{"d":161,"s":"Still","make":"Sing three bridge lines suggested by followers, one take each, and say which you will use.","hook":"Trying your bridge lines, live.","m":6},{"d":162,"s":"Moment","make":"Scroll to the oldest voice memo on your phone. Play it, then play what it became this week.","hook":"A voice memo from years ago, finished.","m":6},{"d":163,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing a lyric where the word stress fights the beat. Then move one word and sing it again so it sits naturally.","hook":"This lyric trips. Here's why.","m":6},{"d":164,"s":"Push","make":"In Logic's arrangement, mute everything, then unmute one track every four bars to show how a song builds.","hook":"A song is deciding who waits.","m":6},{"d":165,"s":"Stack","make":"Record soft oohs in three parts and place them under a verse where strings would go. Mute and unmute them.","hook":"Oohs are the cheapest strings.","m":6},{"d":166,"s":"Finish","make":"A 50-second preview of song two, the slow one. Dark frame, minimal text.","hook":"Song two is the sad one.","m":6},{"d":167,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Offer three possible EP titles on screen and let viewers vote or propose a fourth.","hook":"Name my first EP.","m":6},{"d":168,"s":"Still","make":"Song three as solo piano and voice, before any production. Promise the full version on Friday.","hook":"Song three, before the production.","m":6},{"d":169,"s":"Moment","make":"Record your studio chair creaking and the mic stand clinking. Place both as percussion in song three and point them out.","hook":"The chair squeak made the final mix.","m":6},{"d":170,"s":"Theory","make":"Show two rhymes: a perfect one that sounds predictable and a slant rhyme that sounds like speech.","hook":"Stop rhyming love with above.","m":6},{"d":171,"s":"Push","make":"Show your vocal chain in Logic: EQ, compressor, reverb. Bypass each in turn for two seconds.","hook":"My three-plugin vocal chain.","m":6},{"d":172,"s":"Stack","make":"Play the chorus with a single lead vocal, then with the lead double-tracked and panned.","hook":"Why choruses sound wider.","m":6},{"d":173,"s":"Finish","make":"A 50-second preview of song three with the chair and stand percussion audible.","hook":"Song three has furniture in it.","m":6},{"d":174,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Show three cover designs made from your own photos. Viewers choose.","hook":"Three covers. One EP. You pick.","m":6},{"d":175,"s":"Still","make":"Song four, the worship track, raw at the piano in one take.","hook":"The last song is a prayer.","m":6},{"d":176,"s":"Moment","make":"Play your mix through the phone speaker, cheap earbuds and a car stereo. React honestly to each.","hook":"If it works on this, it works anywhere.","m":6},{"d":177,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing the same chorus in three keys and show where your voice strains and where it sits.","hook":"The right key is the one you can sing.","m":6},{"d":178,"s":"Push","make":"Screen-record the export and the upload to the distributor, including the moment you press submit.","hook":"Uploading my first EP. Hands shaking.","m":6},{"d":179,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing all four choruses back to back with no instruments, joined by one held chord.","hook":"Four choruses, one breath.","m":6},{"d":180,"s":"Finish","make":"Release day. A 60-second medley of the four songs, opening on a screenshot of your profile from Day 1.","hook":"180 days ago I had 46 followers.","m":6},{"d":181,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask which track is each listener's favourite and why, in one word. Play a few seconds of each as a reminder.","hook":"Which track is yours?","m":6},{"d":182,"s":"Still","make":"Improvise a short thank-you song that includes first names from the comments of the release post.","hook":"A thank-you, set to music.","m":6},{"d":183,"s":"Moment","make":"Bring back the Day 1 pressure cooker. Play the Day 1 clip, then play the same idea with everything you have learned.","hook":"Day 1's whistle. Day 183's hands.","m":6},{"d":184,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing one melody in English, Hindi and Telugu. Point out where the vowels change the shape of the tune.","hook":"One melody. Three languages. Which fits?","m":7},{"d":185,"s":"Push","make":"Tap dholak-style patterns on your desk with fingers and palm. Record, slice into a Drum Rack, and play the groove back on pads.","hook":"A dholak made from my desk.","m":7},{"d":186,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing the lead in Hindi and stack the backing vocals in English, answering each line.","hook":"The lead sings Hindi. The choir answers in English.","m":7},{"d":187,"s":"Finish","make":"A 45-second chorus that moves through all three languages, one line each, then all together.","hook":"Three languages in one chorus.","m":7},{"d":188,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask viewers to comment thank you in their own language with the pronunciation. Sing three you already know as the example.","hook":"Teach me thank you in your language.","m":7},{"d":189,"s":"Still","make":"An original Telugu worship chorus, one take, with Hindi and English subtitles.","hook":"Sunday, in Telugu.","m":7},{"d":190,"s":"Moment","make":"Take a saying an elder in your family repeats. Speak it, then sing it as a hook over soul chords.","hook":"My grandmother's saying, as a chorus.","m":7},{"d":191,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a major 7th chord with a raised 4th on top. Then sing the opening phrase of Raag Yaman over it. Same notes.","hook":"Raag Yaman is hiding in this jazz chord.","m":7},{"d":192,"s":"Push","make":"Build a harmonium-like sound in Ableton's Operator from stacked waves with a slow attack. Compare it with a sample.","hook":"Building a harmonium out of sine waves.","m":7},{"d":193,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a short alaap over Sunday-morning gospel chords, then add a low drone voice under it.","hook":"An alaap over Sunday-morning chords.","m":7},{"d":194,"s":"Finish","make":"The family saying becomes a 50-second song with the harmonium sound and desk dholak.","hook":"She said it every day. Now it's a hook.","m":7},{"d":195,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Sing a collage of the thank-yous from Day 188, each with the language name on screen.","hook":"Thank you, in fourteen of your languages.","m":7},{"d":196,"s":"Still","make":"Amazing Grace in your own Hindi translation, one take, with the English line shown beneath each Hindi line.","hook":"An old hymn in my own Hindi words.","m":7},{"d":197,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a wedding procession's brass band from your window. Find its key and play along from your room.","hook":"A baraat passed. I joined from my room.","m":7},{"d":198,"s":"Theory","make":"Speak ta-ka-di-mi over a click to count sixteenth notes, then play a rhythm you learned by speaking it first.","hook":"Counting rhythm the South Indian way.","m":7},{"d":199,"s":"Push","make":"Put a tanpura drone under a gospel progression. Play the chords with and without the drone.","hook":"What a tanpura does to a gospel chord.","m":7},{"d":200,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a run in sargam and the same run in solfege, then stack them together in harmony.","hook":"Sa Re Ga meets Do Re Mi.","m":7},{"d":201,"s":"Finish","make":"A 50-second soul groove built on the baraat recording, starting with the real street sound.","hook":"The wedding wasn't mine. The groove is.","m":7},{"d":202,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Put one line of your chorus on screen and ask viewers to translate it into any language, with pronunciation.","hook":"Translate this line. I'll sing yours.","m":7},{"d":203,"s":"Still","make":"Read a psalm half in Hindi and half in English over quiet piano, speaking rather than singing.","hook":"A psalm, half spoken, half played.","m":7},{"d":204,"s":"Moment","make":"Say a Hindi tongue twister slowly, then at speed, then treat it as a rhythmic vocal riff over a beat.","hook":"A tongue twister is just a drum fill.","m":7},{"d":205,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing a meend between two notes, then play a blues bend between the same two. Put them back to back.","hook":"India and Mississippi bend notes the same way.","m":7},{"d":206,"s":"Push","make":"Walk your street and record five sounds. Load them into one Drum Rack and make a beat from only those.","hook":"Five sounds from one street. One beat.","m":7},{"d":207,"s":"Stack","make":"Record six tracks of claps and a shouted group response to build a qawwali-style chorus behind your lead.","hook":"A one-man qawwali chorus.","m":7},{"d":208,"s":"Finish","make":"The tongue twister becomes 40 seconds of funk, with the twister doubled by the keys.","hook":"Try saying this at 110 bpm.","m":7},{"d":209,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Sing four of the translations from Day 202 in a row, crediting each translator on screen.","hook":"One line, sung in your languages.","m":7},{"d":210,"s":"Still","make":"An original lullaby with one verse each in Telugu, Hindi and English.","hook":"One lullaby, three mother tongues.","m":7},{"d":211,"s":"Moment","make":"Record mustard seeds popping in hot oil. Use the crackle the way producers use vinyl noise under a soft Rhodes.","hook":"Tadka crackle is free vinyl noise.","m":7},{"d":212,"s":"Theory","make":"Sing a Hindi line forced onto an English melody, then rewrite the rhythm so long syllables get long notes.","hook":"Why English tunes break Hindi lyrics.","m":7},{"d":213,"s":"Push","make":"Take the street beat from Day 206, add chords and a sung melody, and show the before and after.","hook":"The street beat got a melody.","m":7},{"d":214,"s":"Stack","make":"With permission, take a follower's solo vocal clip and add three harmony parts around it. Post as a collab.","hook":"A stranger sang. I added the choir.","m":8},{"d":215,"s":"Finish","make":"Write eight bars and hand them to a creator you arranged this with, who adds the next eight. Post your half and tag them.","hook":"I started it. Someone else finishes it.","m":8},{"d":216,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Post a backing track with the chord chart on screen and space for guitar, bass or drums. Invite players to add parts.","hook":"Guitarists, this space is for you.","m":8},{"d":217,"s":"Still","make":"Sing a duet at the piano with a friend or family member, one take.","hook":"Sunday, with company.","m":8},{"d":218,"s":"Moment","make":"Ask a creator in another city to send the sound of their morning. Build a piece from it and post together.","hook":"A morning in another city, sent to me.","m":8},{"d":219,"s":"Theory","make":"Write a chord chart in Nashville numbers on paper. Play it in two different keys from the same chart.","hook":"Numbers every band understands.","m":8},{"d":220,"s":"Push","make":"Screen-record exporting stems with a count-in, naming the files, and zipping the folder.","hook":"How to send a song to a stranger.","m":8},{"d":221,"s":"Stack","make":"Arrange a five-part chord and assign one note each to four followers. Combine their videos with yours in a grid.","hook":"Five people, five rooms, one chord.","m":8},{"d":222,"s":"Finish","make":"Reveal the finished piece from Day 215 with both halves joined. React to their part on camera.","hook":"It came back better than I sent it.","m":8},{"d":223,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Feature the best instrumental additions to the Day 216 track, with each player credited.","hook":"You added the band I don't have.","m":8},{"d":224,"s":"Still","make":"With permission, play a small creator's original song at the piano and tag them.","hook":"A song by someone you should follow.","m":8},{"d":225,"s":"Moment","make":"Record, audio only, a tune hummed by the youngest person in your family. Harmonise it without changing a note.","hook":"A child hummed this. I only added chords.","m":8},{"d":226,"s":"Theory","make":"Accompany a recorded singer badly by overplaying, then well by leaving space. Show three rules as text.","hook":"Accompanying a singer? Play half as much.","m":8},{"d":227,"s":"Push","make":"Take a follower's spoken voice note, with permission, chop it on Push and build a beat from their words.","hook":"Your voice note, remixed.","m":8},{"d":228,"s":"Stack","make":"Trade lines with a singer who sings in a language you do not, then harmonise the last line together.","hook":"Two languages, one conversation.","m":8},{"d":229,"s":"Finish","make":"Finish a full piece with one of the instrumentalists from Day 223. Sixty seconds, both names on screen.","hook":"This guitarist lives 2,000 km away.","m":8},{"d":230,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Post 30 seconds of a rough mix and ask producers what they would change. Promise to apply the top three.","hook":"Producers, tell me what's wrong with this.","m":8},{"d":231,"s":"Still","make":"Record in a real room with other musicians, such as your church band, using only the phone.","hook":"Not my studio. A real room.","m":8},{"d":232,"s":"Moment","make":"Ask a friend who paints, cooks or draws for a timelapse of their work. Score it as if it were a film.","hook":"My friend paints. I score it.","m":8},{"d":233,"s":"Theory","make":"Apply the three most-liked mix suggestions from Day 230. Play before and after for each.","hook":"I took your mixing advice. Listen.","m":8},{"d":234,"s":"Push","make":"Try to jam with another musician over a video call and show the lag. Then show the fix: record separately to a click.","hook":"We tried to jam on a video call.","m":8},{"d":235,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a chorus with the top harmony missing. Put the missing line's notes on screen for viewers to duet.","hook":"Sing the top line. I've got the rest.","m":8},{"d":236,"s":"Finish","make":"A piece built only from sounds and voices followers have sent this month. List the contributors in the caption.","hook":"Every sound here was sent by you.","m":8},{"d":237,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Recommend five small musicians you have learned from, with a few seconds of you playing something each taught you.","hook":"Five musicians smaller than they should be.","m":8},{"d":238,"s":"Still","make":"Play the hymn most requested in your comments this year.","hook":"The hymn you asked for most.","m":8},{"d":239,"s":"Moment","make":"Lay out steel plates, tumblers and tiffin lids. A friend drums on them while you play keys.","hook":"A drum kit from the dish rack.","m":8},{"d":240,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a melody, then write a slower second melody underneath that moves when the first one rests.","hook":"The second melody you never notice.","m":8},{"d":241,"s":"Push","make":"Bundle your everyday sounds into a free pack, share the link in your bio, and show what is inside.","hook":"A year of household sounds. Free. Take them.","m":8},{"d":242,"s":"Stack","make":"Record eight deliberately loose takes of a shouted line and blend them into a crowd.","hook":"How to fake a crowd with one mouth.","m":8},{"d":243,"s":"Finish","make":"Play soul chords under a poet or rapper who speaks their own words. Post as a collab.","hook":"I played. A poet spoke.","m":8},{"d":244,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Feature three things followers made with your free sound pack.","hook":"What you made with my kitchen.","m":8},{"d":245,"s":"Still","make":"An original slow jazz waltz, one take, with a cup of coffee in frame.","hook":"Three beats and a cup of coffee.","m":9},{"d":246,"s":"Moment","make":"Record filter coffee dripping and the tumbler being set down. Play soft swing chords in time with the drip.","hook":"Cafe jazz, minus the cafe.","m":9},{"d":247,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a chord with the root, then lift the root out and add the 9th. Alternate so the change is audible.","hook":"Delete one note. Sound like jazz.","m":9},{"d":248,"s":"Push","make":"Program a brushes kit with swing on Push or choose a brush player in Logic's Drummer. Play chords over it.","hook":"Programming a drummer who uses brushes.","m":9},{"d":249,"s":"Stack","make":"Scat a short phrase using only doo, bah and dn. Stack a second voice a third above.","hook":"Scat singing with only three syllables.","m":9},{"d":250,"s":"Finish","make":"A 50-second cafe piece with the coffee recording, brushes and a scatted melody.","hook":"Brewed and recorded in the same kitchen.","m":9},{"d":251,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask viewers to comment one emoji. Demonstrate with three of your own: play the chord each one sounds like.","hook":"Send an emoji. I'll play how it sounds.","m":9},{"d":252,"s":"Still","make":"Abide With Me as a jazz ballad, one take, with rootless voicings.","hook":"Evening hymn, late-night chords.","m":9},{"d":253,"s":"Moment","make":"Record your footsteps on the stairs. Set the tempo to your walk and play a walking bass line that matches each step.","hook":"My footsteps set the walking bass.","m":9},{"d":254,"s":"Theory","make":"Play only the 3rd and 7th of each chord through a two-five-one. Show how each moves by one step.","hook":"Two notes carry the whole chord.","m":9},{"d":255,"s":"Push","make":"Add tape saturation and a little pitch wobble to a Rhodes part. Toggle it against the clean version.","hook":"Making today sound like 1962.","m":9},{"d":256,"s":"Stack","make":"Record a short piano solo. Write words that fit every note of it and sing them along with the solo.","hook":"I put lyrics on my own solo.","m":9},{"d":257,"s":"Finish","make":"A 50-second swing piece at your walking tempo, footsteps audible in the intro.","hook":"Recorded at walking speed. Literally.","m":9},{"d":258,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play eight of the emojis from Day 251 as chords, three seconds each, with the emoji on screen.","hook":"Your emojis, as chords.","m":9},{"d":259,"s":"Still","make":"An original prayer song over a quiet bossa rhythm, one take.","hook":"A quiet bossa for Sunday.","m":9},{"d":260,"s":"Moment","make":"On a rainy evening, place the mic near the window. Record rain and piano together in the same take.","hook":"It's raining. The mic is by the window.","m":9},{"d":261,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a major two-five-one, then the minor version, and name the one chord quality that changes on each step.","hook":"The sad version of the happiest progression.","m":9},{"d":262,"s":"Push","make":"Play the Charleston rhythm on the pads with one chord, then use it to comp through a progression.","hook":"One rhythm that makes chords swing.","m":9},{"d":263,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a major 6/9 chord one note at a time across five tracks, then play it back together.","hook":"Singing a chord with too many notes.","m":9},{"d":264,"s":"Finish","make":"A 60-second rain piece with the minor two-five-one and the sung 6/9 chord as the last sound.","hook":"The rain played on this record.","m":9},{"d":265,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play a phrase twice, once with a plain voicing and once with an added note. Viewers guess which has it.","hook":"Which one has the secret note?","m":9},{"d":266,"s":"Still","make":"Ask a follower for four notes in advance. Improvise for a minute using only that motif as the seed.","hook":"Four notes from a follower. One improvisation.","m":9},{"d":267,"s":"Moment","make":"Drop ice into a glass and clink a spoon on the rim. Use the clink as the ride cymbal under lounge piano.","hook":"Ice cubes on the ride cymbal.","m":9},{"d":268,"s":"Theory","make":"Take four pop chords and upgrade them in three passes: add 7ths, add a passing chord, substitute one chord.","hook":"Four pop chords, upgraded three times.","m":9},{"d":269,"s":"Push","make":"Record a piano part as MIDI, then drag individual notes in the editor to improve the voicings. Play before and after.","hook":"Fixing my voicings with a mouse.","m":9},{"d":270,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing two bars, answer with two bars of right-hand piano, and keep trading for sixteen bars.","hook":"My voice and my right hand take turns.","m":9},{"d":271,"s":"Finish","make":"A 50-second lounge piece with the glass ride cymbal and the voice-and-piano trades.","hook":"Shaken, stirred and recorded.","m":9},{"d":272,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask viewers to describe a mood in one line, such as Monday at 9 a.m. Play your own example in 20 seconds.","hook":"Describe a mood. I'll play it in jazz.","m":9},{"d":273,"s":"Still","make":"A hymn of your choice with a gentle stride left hand, one take.","hook":"Left hand jumping, right hand praying.","m":9},{"d":274,"s":"Moment","make":"Late at night, record the refrigerator's hum. Find its note and play a nocturne over it.","hook":"The fridge hums. Everything else is asleep.","m":9},{"d":275,"s":"Theory","make":"Draw an energy curve for four songs on paper: medium, high, low, highest. Play five seconds of each in that order.","hook":"How to order four songs.","m":10},{"d":276,"s":"Push","make":"Tour your live-loop template: tracks, loop lengths, which pad does what. Then record one layer to prove it works.","hook":"My whole band fits in this template.","m":10},{"d":277,"s":"Stack","make":"Build a three-part harmony live with the looper, one pass per voice, no stopping and no edits.","hook":"Building a choir live, no safety net.","m":10},{"d":278,"s":"Finish","make":"Your first complete live-loop song in a single unbroken take, 75 seconds.","hook":"One take. Every layer made on camera.","m":10},{"d":279,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Announce your first Instagram Live. Offer two time slots and let viewers pick.","hook":"I'm going live. Pick the day.","m":10},{"d":280,"s":"Still","make":"Go live for 15 minutes. Post the strongest minute as a Reel afterwards.","hook":"The best minute from my first live.","m":10},{"d":281,"s":"Moment","make":"During a live, ask the chat to name an object in your room. Sample it on the spot and loop it into a groove.","hook":"The live chat chose a spoon.","m":10},{"d":282,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the end of one song and move into the next through a chord both keys share, without stopping.","hook":"Changing songs without stopping.","m":10},{"d":283,"s":"Push","make":"Record a loop with a deliberate mistake. Show the undo and re-record on Push, in time, without stopping the music.","hook":"I wrecked the loop. Watch the save.","m":10},{"d":284,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a soft line close to the mic and a loud line from further back. Show the meter staying level.","hook":"Why singers pull the mic away.","m":10},{"d":285,"s":"Finish","make":"A live-loop gospel song in one take with a looped choir and a key change.","hook":"Live-looping a whole church service.","m":10},{"d":286,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Take three requests from comments, limited to hymns, moods or your own songs. Twenty seconds each.","hook":"Three requests, twenty seconds each.","m":10},{"d":287,"s":"Still","make":"From the second live, post the minute where you teach a simple chorus with the lyric on screen.","hook":"Sing this one with me.","m":10},{"d":288,"s":"Moment","make":"Take your phone to a stairwell. Sing and clap there and let viewers hear the natural reverb.","hook":"This stairwell beats my reverb plugin.","m":10},{"d":289,"s":"Theory","make":"Start the same song three ways: cold on the vocal, with a four-bar piano intro, and from a built loop.","hook":"Three ways to start a song.","m":10},{"d":290,"s":"Push","make":"Play finger drums with the left hand and chords with the right, then launch a bass clip, all in one pass.","hook":"Drums, bass and keys. Two hands.","m":10},{"d":291,"s":"Stack","make":"Teach viewers a two-note backing part, then sing the lead over a loop of that part so they can join.","hook":"Your part is two notes. Ready?","m":10},{"d":292,"s":"Finish","make":"A live-loop blues in one take, with a looped shuffle and a verse from Month 5.","hook":"The blues, built one layer at a time.","m":10},{"d":293,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Answer the most-asked question about your looping rig while demonstrating it.","hook":"You asked how the looping works.","m":10},{"d":294,"s":"Still","make":"From the third live, post the opening of your first full four-song set.","hook":"First full set. This is how it opened.","m":10},{"d":295,"s":"Moment","make":"Play a clearly wrong note on purpose. Repeat it, then resolve it so it sounds intended.","hook":"There are no wrong notes. Proof.","m":10},{"d":296,"s":"Theory","make":"End the same song three ways: a ritard, a sudden stop, and a held chord that fades.","hook":"Three ways to end a song.","m":10},{"d":297,"s":"Push","make":"Set up a second angle with the laptop camera. Cut between the two angles in the edit and show the setup.","hook":"Two cameras, one person, zero crew.","m":10},{"d":298,"s":"Stack","make":"Show the coordination exercise you use to sing one rhythm while playing another, including a failed attempt.","hook":"Singing and playing at once is hard.","m":10},{"d":299,"s":"Finish","make":"A 90-second highlight of the full live set, cut from all four songs.","hook":"Ten months of practice, in 90 seconds.","m":10},{"d":300,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Mark Day 300 by asking which piece from the year must go on the year-one record.","hook":"Day 300. Which one stays forever?","m":10},{"d":301,"s":"Still","make":"Everything switched off. Sing unaccompanied in a quiet room, one take.","hook":"Just a voice in a quiet room.","m":10},{"d":302,"s":"Moment","make":"Pick five comments from the past month and sing them, word for word, as a verse.","hook":"Singing my own comment section.","m":10},{"d":303,"s":"Theory","make":"Play one chorus at a whisper and then at full strength. Explain in one line why the loud one needs the quiet one.","hook":"Loud means nothing without quiet.","m":10},{"d":304,"s":"Push","make":"Take the multitrack recording of a live take and do a ten-minute mix. Show before and after.","hook":"Turning a live take into a release.","m":10},{"d":305,"s":"Stack","make":"Loop twelve vocal layers live into one chord, counting each layer on screen.","hook":"Twelve layers. One microphone.","m":10},{"d":306,"s":"Finish","make":"Write a ballad from a message you drafted to someone and never sent. Sixty seconds, piano and voice.","hook":"A text I never sent, sung.","m":11},{"d":307,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Ask viewers to write one line about someone they miss. Share your own line first.","hook":"One line about someone you miss.","m":11},{"d":308,"s":"Still","make":"Build a verse from the lines people shared on Day 307, with no names, and sing it once.","hook":"Your lines. My piano.","m":11},{"d":309,"s":"Moment","make":"Hold an old family photograph to the camera. Play the music you hear when you look at it.","hook":"An old family photo, scored.","m":11},{"d":310,"s":"Theory","make":"Play the ballad left hand: root, fifth, tenth, rolled slowly. Show it alone, then under a melody.","hook":"The left-hand pattern behind every ballad.","m":11},{"d":311,"s":"Push","make":"Write three string lines in Logic over a piano part: a high held note, a middle counter-line, a low cello. Add them one at a time.","hook":"Adding strings without a string section.","m":11},{"d":312,"s":"Stack","make":"Record four very soft head-voice harmonies and tuck them under the lead until they are almost inaudible. Mute them to show the loss.","hook":"Harmonies you feel more than hear.","m":11},{"d":313,"s":"Finish","make":"The photograph piece as a 60-second ballad with strings and soft harmonies. Open on the photo.","hook":"Everyone in this photo is in the song.","m":11},{"d":314,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Play a short dedication piece and ask viewers to send it to the person it makes them think of.","hook":"Send this to who it's for.","m":11},{"d":315,"s":"Still","make":"An original first-dance song for a couple, one take, simple enough that others could use it.","hook":"For two people on their first dance.","m":11},{"d":316,"s":"Moment","make":"Record a parent or elder telling a ten-second memory. Play gently underneath their voice, following their pauses.","hook":"My father told a story. I played under it.","m":11},{"d":317,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a phrase that should resolve to chord I, and land on chord vi instead. Then play the expected ending.","hook":"The chord that refuses to go home.","m":11},{"d":318,"s":"Push","make":"Automate a delay so only the last word of a line echoes. Show the automation lane.","hook":"Making one word echo.","m":11},{"d":319,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing a line low and the same line an octave higher, perfectly together, with no other harmony.","hook":"Same note, an octave apart.","m":11},{"d":320,"s":"Finish","make":"A 60-second ballad built around the elder's recorded story, with their voice opening and closing it.","hook":"His story. My chords.","m":11},{"d":321,"s":"Your Turn","make":"With permission, take one story sent by DM and write 20 seconds of score for it. Show the story as anonymous text.","hook":"You sent a story. I wrote its music.","m":11},{"d":322,"s":"Still","make":"A gentle song of comfort for people who are grieving, one take, no production.","hook":"For anyone carrying something heavy.","m":11},{"d":323,"s":"Moment","make":"Record the sound of your empty room. Write a piece with long rests and let the room tone fill them.","hook":"I recorded silence. Then left it in.","m":11},{"d":324,"s":"Theory","make":"Hold one bass note while the right-hand chords change above it for eight bars, then finally move the bass.","hook":"The bass refuses to move.","m":11},{"d":325,"s":"Push","make":"Record a vocal very close to the mic at low gain, add a de-esser and a little compression. Compare with a normal take.","hook":"Why this vocal sounds an inch away.","m":11},{"d":326,"s":"Stack","make":"Build a sentence one word per layer: each new voice adds the next word until the full line is sung in harmony.","hook":"Each voice adds one word.","m":11},{"d":327,"s":"Finish","make":"The silence piece as a 50-second ballad, with rests long enough to feel risky.","hook":"The quietest thing I've made.","m":11},{"d":328,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Put your verse two on screen and invite viewers to rewrite it. The best version gets sung.","hook":"Verse two is yours to rewrite.","m":11},{"d":329,"s":"Still","make":"A lullaby written for adults, slow and low, one take.","hook":"A lullaby for tired grown-ups.","m":11},{"d":330,"s":"Moment","make":"Write a letter to yourself a year from now. Film the page and sing three lines from it.","hook":"A letter to me, one year from now.","m":11},{"d":331,"s":"Theory","make":"Play a phrase strictly in time, then with rubato, stretching the important notes.","hook":"Why ballads ignore the metronome.","m":11},{"d":332,"s":"Push","make":"Ride the vocal fader by hand through a whole verse, recording the moves, instead of using a compressor.","hook":"Riding one fader like it's 1975.","m":11},{"d":333,"s":"Stack","make":"Start with one voice and double the count every bar until sixteen voices are singing.","hook":"From one voice to sixteen.","m":11},{"d":334,"s":"Finish","make":"Your largest arrangement so far: a 90-second ballad with strings, bass, harmonies and a stripped final line.","hook":"The biggest thing I've ever arranged.","m":11},{"d":335,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Sing the winning rewrite of verse two from Day 328, crediting the writer on screen.","hook":"Your verse two beat mine.","m":11},{"d":336,"s":"Still","make":"Re-record the Day 7 hymn in the same frame. Show both takes side by side.","hook":"Day 7 on the left. Today on the right.","m":12},{"d":337,"s":"Moment","make":"Make one groove from every household object used this year, each appearing for a beat or two with its day number.","hook":"Every object from this year, one groove.","m":12},{"d":338,"s":"Theory","make":"Rank the five theory ideas that changed your playing most, with two seconds of each.","hook":"Five ideas that actually changed my playing.","m":12},{"d":339,"s":"Push","make":"Open the Day 10 beat project and play it. Then play this week's beat.","hook":"My first beat versus my latest.","m":12},{"d":340,"s":"Stack","make":"Rebuild the Day 4 line with twenty voices and show a counter climbing on screen.","hook":"Day 4 had three voices. Count these.","m":12},{"d":341,"s":"Finish","make":"Reveal the first track of the year-one record, 60 seconds, with the tracklist on screen.","hook":"Track one of the year-one record.","m":12},{"d":342,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Show the track titles and ask viewers to propose the running order.","hook":"Put my record in order.","m":12},{"d":343,"s":"Still","make":"Speak for a minute about what showing up daily taught you, playing quietly underneath.","hook":"What a year of showing up taught me.","m":12},{"d":344,"s":"Moment","make":"Ask followers to send a sound from their home with their city. Build a groove and label each sound's origin.","hook":"Sounds from eleven cities, one song.","m":12},{"d":345,"s":"Theory","make":"Show three things you still cannot play, attempted honestly, and say they are next year's list.","hook":"Things I still can't play.","m":12},{"d":346,"s":"Push","make":"A fast tour of every piece of equipment and what it did this year, one item per two seconds.","hook":"Everything I used this year, in 40 seconds.","m":12},{"d":347,"s":"Stack","make":"Teach the finale chorus in three labelled parts so viewers can learn one before Day 365.","hook":"Learn this. You'll need it on Day 365.","m":12},{"d":348,"s":"Finish","make":"Reveal the trilingual track from the record, 60 seconds.","hook":"The song that needed three languages.","m":12},{"d":349,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Invite followers to record themselves singing the finale chorus and send or remix it.","hook":"Record the chorus. You're in the finale.","m":12},{"d":350,"s":"Still","make":"The record's worship track, stripped to piano and voice, one take.","hook":"The record's quietest track, unplugged.","m":12},{"d":351,"s":"Moment","make":"Record one sound each hour for a full day. Arrange them in order into 30 seconds of music.","hook":"One whole day, as 30 seconds of music.","m":12},{"d":352,"s":"Theory","make":"Give three rules for anyone who wants to start their own 365 days of music.","hook":"Want to do this? Three rules.","m":12},{"d":353,"s":"Push","make":"Cut together the year's failed takes, crashes and wrong notes.","hook":"Every time it went wrong.","m":12},{"d":354,"s":"Stack","make":"Assemble the follower recordings from Day 349 into a choir and show the grid of faces, with permission.","hook":"This choir is sixty strangers.","m":12},{"d":355,"s":"Finish","make":"Release the year-one record. A 75-second medley with every track title.","hook":"365 days. Nine songs. It's out.","m":12},{"d":356,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Hold a listening party on Live and post the best reactions.","hook":"Your first reactions to the record.","m":12},{"d":357,"s":"Still","make":"An original benediction song to close the year of Sundays, one take.","hook":"A blessing to end the year.","m":12},{"d":358,"s":"Moment","make":"Plot your follower count across the year and play the curve as a melody, low to high.","hook":"My follower graph is a melody.","m":12},{"d":359,"s":"Theory","make":"Play one chord for each month of the year, the chord that defined it, in order.","hook":"Twelve months, twelve chords.","m":12},{"d":360,"s":"Push","make":"A timelapse of building the finale arrangement, from empty project to full session.","hook":"Building the last song.","m":12},{"d":361,"s":"Stack","make":"Sing the handles of your earliest followers as a harmonised roll call.","hook":"My first fifty followers, sung by name.","m":12},{"d":362,"s":"Finish","make":"The first half of the finale song, stopping right before the chorus the followers sing.","hook":"Half the finale. The rest on Day 365.","m":12},{"d":363,"s":"Your Turn","make":"Offer three directions for year two and ask viewers to choose.","hook":"Year two. You decide.","m":12},{"d":364,"s":"Still","make":"Finish the four-line chorus you wrote on Day 14 as a complete song and sing it once.","hook":"I started this song on Day 14.","m":12},{"d":365,"s":"Moment","make":"The finale: a full live performance with the follower choir, the year's everyday sounds and the pressure cooker's whistle as the last note.","hook":"Day 365 of 365.","m":12}]};